From f58885ae1ce0a2939206c5f38ba30cc363298dc1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dan Hamik <dan@hamik.net>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 13:25:05 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] tokens: sign the device in again when its session has expired

The keyring keeps this device's Passport STS token under device-tokens,
and login.live.com issues it for 60 days. get_device_sts_token handed it
out without reading its wsu:Expires, and ensure_device_credentials only
signed the device in again when the entry was missing, so once the token
lapsed every request it signed was answered with a SOAP Fault -- for as
long as the keyring kept it. exchange_device_token had no arm for a
Fault, and a license request ended on

  not implemented: Exchange token supports only singular token right
  now Fault(Fault)

Deleting device-tokens by hand, and nothing else, was enough: the next
run signed the same device in again and the license request went
through.

Read the lifetime: get_device_sts_token and get_user_sts_token report
TokenStoreError::Expired for a legacy token within five minutes of its
wsu:Expires (a lifetime that does not parse is used as before). For the
device token that makes ensure_device_credentials drop device-tokens and
sign the device in again before anything uses it. The account's token
(user-tokens, 90 days) can only be replaced by a sign-in, and the
license request now says so: "not logged in: ... has expired ...; sign
in again".

A device session can also be refused before its stored lifetime ends.
exchange_device_token turns a Fault into RSTError::Fault, carrying the
pp reqstatus when there is one, instead of panicking, and the license
request answers it by dropping device-tokens (dev_license, user-DA and
user-tokens stay), signing the device in again through the new
reauthenticate_device_session, and retrying once. A second refusal is
reported, not retried.

Reported downstream as
https://github.com/Wyze3306/BedrockOnLinux/issues/367.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
